  "notes": "Web 454 — CLOSED-DAY-CORRECTION-DURABILITY (Nov 13, 2026; layers on web 453). Web 453 gave a closed day its −1/+1 door and the tap did land — the Manifest repainted the corrected figure — but a browser pass on the live 453 build proved the correction did not survive a reload: Nov 13 read 5, one tap showed 4, a reload read 5 again. Root cause, proven in a new bench (two page lives over one store and one cloud, the real geoloads driving the tap and the real cloudsync running every pull, push and merge): the honest push and pull legs were never broken; the sync merge's same-lineage arbitration for geoloads days runs on raw updatedAt alone — a stamp every engine/state re-persist bumps — so a later-stamped copy of the day whose facts PREDATE the correction (an evening re-persist of the uncorrected record, or a twin stamped on a skewed clock) outvoted the correction in both the pull and the push merge, and the stale correction was re-taught to every surface. Fix: (1) js/geoloads.js — the two record-writing correction doors (GeoLoads.nudgeDate, and nudge's not-watching branch) stamp each correction with corrAt (its own stamp, minted ahead of every stamp the record already carries — max(now, updatedAt+1, corrAt+1), the v445 idiom at record level) and corrFrom (the value it replaced); only the correction fields move on the record, exactly as before. (2) js/cloudsync.js — mergeTelemetryDays' geoloads same-lineage branch keeps the v293 stamp winner for the record, but when the two copies' corr differs it arbitrates the corr by that evidence: both sides stamped → the later corrAt donates (a later genuine correction still wins); one side stamped → it donates when the other side's corr is exactly its corrFrom (the other side is the pre-correction state of the day, however fresh its updatedAt). Counts that differ keep the v224 totals law; livemiles untouched; counting rules, the peg law, live-day behaviour, close/Revenue logic untouched. NO NATIVE CHANGE.",
